WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.860 --> 00:00:02.360
"Liftoff on Apollo 11..."

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:07.820
"Tranquility base, the eagle has landed."

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:10.400
50 years ago, Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.120
became the first humans to ever land on the moon.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:16.800
One small step for man, one giant leap for mankind,

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:17.600
that whole thing.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:19.620
Astronauts also brought back

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.020
the first samples of the moon.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.560
They brought back rocks and soil

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:23.920
from the lunar surface.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:25.440
"These boxes include

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:28.720
the samples of various types of rock,

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:32.980
the ground mass of the soil, the sand and silt..."

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.520
Having Apollo samples here on the ground

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:36.860
where we can study them

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:39.640
changed entire field of planetary science.

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:41.760
It's revealed stuff, not only about the moon,

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.020
but about how other planetary surfaces

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:45.360
change and evolve.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.200
And we continue to study them today.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.160
They're still revealing secrets about the moon itself

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.060
and the rest of the solar system.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:54.600
NASA keeps most of those samples

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.960
at the NASA Johnson Space Center in Houston

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:58.800
in a secure, pristine clean room...

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.160
that I got to go visit.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:09.900
So I showed up at the lab, and I met Charisse Krysher.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:12.880
What I do is I prepare Apollo moon rock samples

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:15.680
for our scientists out in the community

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:17.040
so that they can do research.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:18.760
They keep all the rocks in a cleanroom

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.900
and for the lucky people who get to go in there

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:23.920
the price of admission is putting on this crazy suit

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:27.280
that's supposed to keep all of your human stuff

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:29.040
from getting anywhere near the rocks.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:39.720
[music]

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:41.800
The last step was an air shower.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:43.800
Please watch your step.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:45.900
It actually felt more like a light breeze.

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:52.600
[Elevator music]

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:53.940
There's moon rocks out there.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:56.160
There are a bunch of clean rooms at NASA Johnson,

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:59.080
but this one is called the Lunar Pristine Samples Lab.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:01.120
That's because the moon rocks stored here

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.300
have not been exposed to Earth air at all

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:06.200
since they arrived back on Earth in the 60s and 70s.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.940
Here you have a lot of water in the air,

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.900
and you have a lot of oxygen in the air.

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:13.800
And as soon as a rock is exposed to the Earth's surface,

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.000
it gets attacked by those two things,

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.280
and the minerals inside those rocks

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:18.880
start to change immediately.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:21.760
The pristine rocks are kept in these special cabinets

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:25.060
that are filled with pure nitrogen instead of earth air.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:26.720
And they have a higher pressure on the inside

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:27.620
than the outside.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:29.720
So if air ever escapes,

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:31.660
it comes out and away from the rocks

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:33.200
instead of things going in

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:34.900
to potentially contaminate the rocks.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:38.900
It feels like it's hugging my arms really tight in there.

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:42.780
That's the pressure? Alright. Cool.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:43.700
[ding]

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:49.880
Only three materials can come into
contact with these rocks.

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:52.360
They're aluminum, Teflon and stainless steel.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:54.440
So first you have your gloves in your bunny suit,

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:57.260
then you have the gloves that let
you reach into the cabinet,

00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:59.080
and if you want to pick up a sample directly,

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:01.480
you have to put on Teflon gloves on top of that.

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:04.600
So what the lunar sample processors do in this room

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:07.320
is they prepare rocks to send out to researchers

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:08.520
who can then study them.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:11.280
To do that, they have to break off little chips of the rocks

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:12.800
Sometimes they use buzz saws.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.820
Sometimes they use a hammer and a chisel.

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:17.420
Sometimes they just scoop up some soil and weigh it.

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:18.840
And then they can package it up

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:19.980
and send it off into the world.

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:21.280
I used to work in a lab

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:23.380
where we worked with pictures of Mars

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:25.420
from the Mars rovers Spirit and Opportunity.

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:27.560
My job was to process pictures of dirt

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:29.680
as they were sent back to Earth.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:32.600
And all I wanted was to scoop up some of that Mars dirt

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:34.900
and just let it run through my fingers.

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:37.020
I figured this was the closest I could get

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:40.200
to actually touching some extraterrestrial space dirt.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.660
Am I allowed to reach in and hold the thing or no?

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.900
[Sigh] Um I'm afraid not.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:47.900
Can we do it?

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:49.380
Uh we cannot.

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:52.160
Unfortunately, no one is allowed to touch

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:54.100
the moon rocks in the pristine lab.

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:56.460
That's the whole point of the pristine lab.

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:58.240
But I did get to hold a sample

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:00.580
that was encased in this acrylic trophy,

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:02.000
which was pretty darn good.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.340
This is a moon rock... in my hand!

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:06.900
When the processors are ready

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:08.900
to send a sample out to a researcher,

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:10.560
they pack them up really carefully,

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:11.700
and then they just ship them out

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:12.860
through the regular mail.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:15.340
They don't label it 'this is a moon rock' or anything.

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:16.560
They don't label them fragile.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:17.900
And they don't even insure them

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:19.200
because what would you insure it for?

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:20.420
They're priceless.

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:21.680
When samples come back,

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:23.400
researchers mail them back.

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:25.120
And they're no longer pristine

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:26.060
because they've been outside

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:27.480
of those nitrogen cabinets.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:29.020
Those rocks are mostly available

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:30.700
to use again in other studies.

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:32.380
The processors keep very careful track

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:34.360
of everything that has ever happened to them.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:37.420
So that researchers know whether previous experiments

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:39.540
can affect their current experiments.

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:42.440
At least a little bit of every single sample

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:44.140
has been analyzed.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:49.080
We have roughly about 80% of our collection

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:51.620
is still considered pristine.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:53.700
Um we have about...

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:57.620
about 5% is out display, on display.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:01.840
Another 5% is out to our scientists around the world.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:04.720
And another 15% of each sample

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:08.800
is in a remote storage facility at another NASA center

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:11.860
just in case something happened to this facility

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:13.600
and we lost the samples in this facility.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:15.600
One of the things these rocks have told us

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:18.480
is that the moon probably formed in a giant impact

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:21.740
where the early Earth, when it was still hot molten

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:23.600
four and a half billion years ago,

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:25.280
collided with another protoplanet.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:27.000
That might have been about the size of Mars.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.960
The debris from that collision splashed out into space

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:31.560
and congealed to form the moon.

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:33.440
"...I'm picking up several pieces

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:36.200
of really vesicular rock our here now."

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:37.320
At the end of Apollo 11,

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:39.000
when Neil Armstrong was finishing up

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:40.840
the first moonwalk ever,

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.460
he was packing up the rocks into this sample box

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:44.960
that they were going to bring back to Earth.

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:46.780
And he saw that there was some space left in it,

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:49.020
and he didn't want the big rocks to jostle around,

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:50.600
so he threw in some scoops of soil

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:51.900
to kind of cushion them.

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:54.180
Best idea that he could have had

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:57.920
because they ended up bringing back so much sample

00:05:57.920 --> 00:05:59.940
that there were a lot of outlying studies

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:00.680
that could be done

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:02.980
that wouldn't have gotten samples otherwise.

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:04.940
The soils from Apollo 11 contained

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:07.940
these little white rocks called anorthosites.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:09.660
These are not very dense.

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:10.920
These are really light rocks.

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:12.340
And scientists thought that maybe

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:14.340
they had floated to the surface

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:16.440
of an ancient lunar magma ocean,

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:17.820
and formed a crust

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:20.340
that used to cover most of the moon.

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:24.920
Over the years, instruments get better,

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:27.840
new instrumentation is invented,

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:30.760
new techniques come about.

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:32.460
There are questions we can answer now

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:35.120
that scientists didn't even know to ask in the 70s.

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:37.380
So keeping these samples pristine

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:39.600
will help scientists for the next 50 years

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:42.160
answer questions that we haven't even thought of today.

00:06:43.360 --> 00:07:31.280
[music]

